Chapter: GopUsb
  cites Works chapter GopXhci
  cites Kernel chapter Pci
  cites Works chapter GopUsbKbd
  cites Works chapter GopUsbMouse
  cites Works chapter GopUsbMsc
  cites Works chapter GopAhci
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 One enumeration of the USB bus for the whole boot (H2). kbd-connect
 and msc-connect each begin with a controller reset, so they are
 alternatives, not housemates: whichever runs second destroys the
 first's device state -- on real hardware, where the boot medium is a
 USB stick AND the keyboard is USB, that is a dead keyboard the
 moment a storage screen runs. usb-attach brings the controller up
 once, walks the root ports once, classifies each device by the
 interfaces in its configuration descriptor, and configures the boot
 keyboard and the mass-storage disk on the same host. The disk handle
 is then published to the fixed cell block (GopUsbMsc), so GopDisk's
 stateless dispatch transfers on the running host instead of
 reconnecting.

 A root port may also hold a hub: real laptops route their built-in
 ports through internal hubs, so a keyboard that enumerates fine on
 QEMU's root port simply is not there on the machine this arc exists
 for. A device whose configuration carries a hub interface (class 9)
 is brought up as one -- configured, declared to the controller,
 ports powered -- and each connected downstream port is reset and
 enumerated with the route string extended by one nibble. The walk
 recurses through nested hubs until the route string's five nibbles
 are spent.

 Every device shares one event ring; the per-endpoint latch in GopXhci
 (xhci-wait-xfer) is what keeps a storage wait from eating a keyboard
 completion, and one interface of a combo device from eating its
 sibling's.

 We say:

Section: Attachment

  UsbAttach = record {
    ua-kbd : UsbKbd,
    ua-mouse : UsbMouse,
    ua-disk : MscDisk
  }

  UsbFound = record {
    uf-kbd : UsbKbd,
    uf-mouse : UsbMouse,
    uf-disk : MscDisk
  }

 A machine may carry more than one xHCI, and the boot stick is not
 obliged to be on the first: the ASUS has an Intel PCH controller whose
 four ports are all Full or Low speed and an ASMedia 1b21:1242 behind a
 bridge, and the stick is on the ASMedia one. Taking the first controller
 and stopping reports no disk while looking at a controller the disk was
 never on -- an answer that reads as "our storage stack fails on real
 hardware" and means "we did not look there". So every xHCI function in
 the bus tree is brought up and walked until the keyboard, the mouse and
 the disk have all been found, and each controller records its own
 outcome rather than overwriting the last one's.

  usb-max-hosts : Integer = 8

  usb-attach : [Device.Port] UsbAttach
  usb-attach = act
    scan <- pci-scan-all
    usb-attach-all scan
  end

  usb-attach-all : PciScanResult -> [Device.Port] UsbAttach
  usb-attach-all (scan) = act
    z <- xdiag-zero 0 96
    c <- xdiag-put 40 (pci-count-class scan xhci-pci-class xhci-pci-subclass)
    usb-attach-with scan (alloc-zeroed (xdiag-span * 4) 64)
  end

  usb-attach-with : PciScanResult, Integer -> [Device.Port] UsbAttach
  usb-attach-with (scan) (shadow) = act
    f <- usb-hosts scan 0 0 shadow (UsbFound { uf-kbd = kbd-no-kbd, uf-mouse = mouse-no-mouse, uf-disk = msc-no-disk })
    let r = if xdiag-get 44 == 0 then 0 else xdiag-load shadow
    in usb-done f
  end

  usb-hosts : PciScanResult, Integer, Integer, Integer, UsbFound -> [Device.Port] UsbFound
  usb-hosts (scan) (from) (ord) (shadow) (found) = act
    if ord >= usb-max-hosts then found
    else if usb-found-all found then found
    else usb-hosts-at scan (pci-index-class scan xhci-pci-class xhci-pci-subclass from) ord shadow found
  end

  usb-hosts-at : PciScanResult, Integer, Integer, Integer, UsbFound -> [Device.Port] UsbFound
  usb-hosts-at (scan) (idx) (ord) (shadow) (found) = act
    if idx < 0 then found
    else usb-hosts-step scan idx ord shadow found
  end

  usb-hosts-step : PciScanResult, Integer, Integer, Integer, UsbFound -> [Device.Port] UsbFound
  usb-hosts-step (scan) (idx) (ord) (shadow) (found) = act
    f2 <- usb-host-one (list-at (scan.devices) idx) ord shadow found
    usb-hosts scan (idx + 1) (ord + 1) shadow f2
  end

  usb-host-one : PciDevice, Integer, Integer, UsbFound -> [Device.Port] UsbFound
  usb-host-one (d) (ord) (shadow) (found) = act
    xh <- xhci-connect-one d ord
    if xh.xh-ok == False then found
    else usb-host-walk xh ord shadow found
  end

  usb-host-walk : XhciHost, Integer, Integer, UsbFound -> UsbFound
  usb-host-walk (xh) (ord) (shadow) (found) =
    let o = xdiag-put 41 (xdiag-get 41 + 1)
    in let cur = xdiag-put xhci-cur-ord (ord + 1)
    in let w = xdiag-put 42 (bit-or (xdiag-get 42) (bit-shl 1 ord))
    in let after = usb-walk xh 0 found
    in let m = xhci-ctl-put ord 3 (bit-xor (usb-found-mask after) (usb-found-mask found))
    in let c1 = usb-credit 43 ((found.uf-disk).md-ok) ((after.uf-disk).md-ok) ord
    in let c2 = usb-credit 44 ((found.uf-kbd).uk-ok) ((after.uf-kbd).uk-ok) ord
    in let s = if ((found.uf-kbd).uk-ok == False) & ((after.uf-kbd).uk-ok) then xdiag-save shadow else 0
    in after

  usb-credit : Integer, Boolean, Boolean, Integer -> Integer
  usb-credit (cell) (was) (now) (ord) =
    if was then 0
    else if now == False then 0
    else xdiag-put cell (ord + 1)

  usb-found-mask : UsbFound -> Integer
  usb-found-mask (found) =
    let k = if (found.uf-kbd).uk-ok then 1 else 0
    in let m = if (found.uf-mouse).um-ok then 2 else 0
    in let d = if (found.uf-disk).md-ok then 4 else 0
    in bit-or k (bit-or m d)

  usb-found-all : UsbFound -> Boolean
  usb-found-all (found) =
    if (found.uf-kbd).uk-ok == False then False
    else if (found.uf-mouse).um-ok == False then False
    else (found.uf-disk).md-ok

Section: The Walk

 The first keyboard becomes the primary handle and every later
 boot-keyboard interface is bound as a peer on it; first mouse wins,
 first disk wins; a port whose device is none of them (a camera) is
 opened, inspected, and left addressed -- slots are plentiful and
 abandoning one is harmless. The walk publishes the disk handle at the
 end, after everything on the shared host is configured.

  usb-done : UsbFound -> UsbAttach
  usb-done (found) =
    let p = if (found.uf-disk).md-ok then msc-publish (found.uf-disk) else 0
    in UsbAttach { ua-kbd = found.uf-kbd, ua-mouse = found.uf-mouse, ua-disk = found.uf-disk }

  usb-walk : XhciHost, Integer, UsbFound -> UsbFound
  usb-walk (xh) (from) (found) =
    let port = xhci-find-port xh from
    in if port < 0 then found
    else let speed = xhci-reset-port xh port
    in if speed == 0 then usb-walk xh (port + 1) found
    else let dev = xhci-open-device xh port speed
    in if dev.ud-ok == False then usb-walk xh (port + 1) found
    else usb-walk xh (port + 1) (usb-inspect xh dev 0 0 found)

 One addressed device, classified by its configuration descriptor.
 The route and depth name where it sits: route is the slot-context
 route string that reached it, depth the count of hubs above it --
 the nibble index its own children would occupy, were it a hub.

 A device carrying any boot HID interface is classified PER INTERFACE:
 a combo device -- a wireless dongle is the canonical case -- carries a
 boot keyboard on one interface and a boot mouse on another, and the
 old whole-device classification handed the entire device to whichever
 class its first interface matched, so the mouse half of every combo
 was unreachable. The ASUS proved the keyboard version of this on the
 glass (four keyboard-shaped interfaces, keys on the fourth bound);
 the mouse gets the identical treatment before it costs a flash.
 Devices with no boot HID keep the old shape: mass storage first-wins,
 then hubs descend. A device that is both HID and hub descends too,
 with SET_CONFIGURATION sent once for the whole device.

  usb-inspect : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, UsbFound -> UsbFound
  usb-inspect (xh) (dev) (route) (depth) (found) =
    let cfg = alloc-zeroed 256 64
    in let g = usb-get-descriptor xh dev 2 0 cfg 255
    in if g == False then found
    else let total = kbd-cfg-total cfg
    in let hid = usb-has-boot-hid cfg 0 total
    in let f2 = if hid then usb-hid-walk xh dev cfg 0 total 0 found else found
    in let f3 = usb-inspect-disk xh dev cfg total hid f2
    in let hi = hub-find-interface cfg 0 total
    in if hi < 0 then f3
    else if depth >= usb-max-depth then f3
    else usb-open-hub xh dev cfg hi total route depth hid f3

  usb-inspect-disk : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Boolean, UsbFound -> UsbFound
  usb-inspect-disk (xh) (dev) (cfg) (total) (hid) (found) =
    if hid then found
    else if (found.uf-disk).md-ok then found
    else let mi = msc-find-interface cfg 0 total
    in if mi < 0 then found
    else UsbFound { uf-kbd = found.uf-kbd, uf-mouse = found.uf-mouse, uf-disk = msc-open-iface xh dev cfg mi total }

Section: The HID Interface Walk

 Only alternate setting zero is considered (byte 3 of the interface
 descriptor): an interface's alternate settings repeat its number with
 different endpoint sets, and binding two settings of one interface
 would drive one pipe from two rings.

  usb-has-boot-hid : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  usb-has-boot-hid (cfg) (off) (total) =
    if off >= total then False
    else let len = peek-byte cfg off
    in if len == 0 then False
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 1) /= 4 then usb-has-boot-hid cfg (off + len) total
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 3) /= 0 then usb-has-boot-hid cfg (off + len) total
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 5) /= 3 then usb-has-boot-hid cfg (off + len) total
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 6) /= 1 then usb-has-boot-hid cfg (off + len) total
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 7) == 1 then True
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 7) == 2 then True
    else usb-has-boot-hid cfg (off + len) total

 The walk threads `cfgd`: SET_CONFIGURATION is sent by the first bind
 on the device and never again (USB 2.0 9.1.1.5 -- re-sending it resets
 every data toggle the device holds). Boot keyboards join the keyboard
 peer set, boot mice the mouse peer set folding into the primary's
 position block, and every OTHER HID interrupt-IN interface on a device
 that carries boot HID is bound as a raw counting listener on the
 keyboard set -- pumped and counted, never decoded, never fed to the
 mailbox -- because gaming devices commonly route their real traffic to
 an interface whose layout only the report descriptor defines, and a
 raw listener's non-zero count on the glass is what says so.

  usb-hid-walk : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, UsbFound -> UsbFound
  usb-hid-walk (xh) (dev) (cfg) (off) (total) (cfgd) (found) =
    if off >= total then found
    else let len = peek-byte cfg off
    in if len == 0 then found
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 1) /= 4 then usb-hid-walk xh dev cfg (off + len) total cfgd found
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 3) /= 0 then usb-hid-walk xh dev cfg (off + len) total cfgd found
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 5) /= 3 then usb-hid-walk xh dev cfg (off + len) total cfgd found
    else let ep = kbd-find-int-in cfg (off + len) total
    in if ep < 0 then usb-hid-walk xh dev cfg (off + len) total cfgd found
    else let sub = peek-byte cfg (off + 6)
    in let proto = peek-byte cfg (off + 7)
    in if if sub == 1 then proto == 1 else False then usb-hid-bind-kbd xh dev cfg off ep total cfgd found False
    else if if sub == 1 then proto == 2 else False then usb-hid-bind-mouse xh dev cfg off ep total cfgd found
    else usb-hid-bind-kbd xh dev cfg off ep total cfgd found True

  usb-hid-bind-kbd : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, UsbFound, Boolean -> UsbFound
  usb-hid-bind-kbd (xh) (dev) (cfg) (off) (ep) (total) (cfgd) (found) (raw) =
    let len = peek-byte cfg off
    in if raw & ((found.uf-kbd).uk-ok == False) then usb-hid-walk xh dev cfg (off + len) total cfgd found
    else let k = kbd-open-iface xh dev (peek-byte cfg (off + 2)) (peek-byte cfg 5) (bit-and (peek-byte cfg (ep + 2)) 15) (kbd-ep-maxpkt cfg ep) (peek-byte cfg (ep + 6)) (cfgd == 0) (peek-byte cfg (off + 6) == 1) raw
    in if k.uk-ok == False then usb-hid-walk xh dev cfg (off + len) total cfgd found
    else let n = usb-hid-note xh dev (k.uk-dci) (k.uk-raw) False (k.uk-report)
    in if (found.uf-kbd).uk-ok then
      let a = kbd-adopt (found.uf-kbd) k
      in usb-hid-walk xh dev cfg (off + len) total 1 found
    else usb-hid-walk xh dev cfg (off + len) total 1 (UsbFound { uf-kbd = k, uf-mouse = found.uf-mouse, uf-disk = found.uf-disk })

  usb-hid-bind-mouse : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, UsbFound -> UsbFound
  usb-hid-bind-mouse (xh) (dev) (cfg) (off) (ep) (total) (cfgd) (found) =
    let len = peek-byte cfg off
    in let havem = (found.uf-mouse).um-ok
    in let pos = if havem then (found.uf-mouse).um-pos else 0
    in let m = mouse-open-iface xh dev (peek-byte cfg (off + 2)) (peek-byte cfg 5) (bit-and (peek-byte cfg (ep + 2)) 15) (kbd-ep-maxpkt cfg ep) (peek-byte cfg (ep + 6)) (cfgd == 0) pos
    in if m.um-ok == False then usb-hid-walk xh dev cfg (off + len) total cfgd found
    else let n = usb-hid-note xh dev (m.um-dci) False True (m.um-report)
    in if havem then
      let a = mouse-adopt (found.uf-mouse) m
      in usb-hid-walk xh dev cfg (off + len) total 1 found
    else usb-hid-walk xh dev cfg (off + len) total 1 (UsbFound { uf-kbd = found.uf-kbd, uf-mouse = m, uf-disk = found.uf-disk })

 Cell 47 counts every HID interface bound this boot; rows at 80, 84,
 88, 92 carry the first four. Per row: the device descriptor's idVendor
 and idProduct, the root port and route string, then the DCI with the
 raw flag at bit 8, the mouse flag at bit 9 and the speed class at bits
 15:12, then the report buffer's address. The row is written at bind
 time so a boot that dies later still photographs with its devices
 named.

  usb-hid-note : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Boolean, Boolean, Integer -> Integer
  usb-hid-note (xh) (dev) (dci) (raw) (mouse) (buf) =
    let idx = xdiag-get 47
    in let c = xdiag-put 47 (idx + 1)
    in if idx >= 4 then 0
    else let dbuf = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in let g = usb-get-descriptor xh dev 1 0 dbuf 18
    in let vid = if g then bit-or (peek-byte dbuf 8) (bit-shl (peek-byte dbuf 9) 8) else 0
    in let pid = if g then bit-or (peek-byte dbuf 10) (bit-shl (peek-byte dbuf 11) 8) else 0
    in let base = 80 + idx * 4
    in let w0 = xdiag-put base (bit-or (bit-shl vid 16) pid)
    in let w1 = xdiag-put (base + 1) (bit-or (bit-shl (dev.ud-port) 24) (bit-and (dev.ud-route) #FFFFF))
    in let w2 = xdiag-put (base + 2) (bit-or dci (bit-or (if raw then 256 else 0) (bit-or (if mouse then 512 else 0) (bit-shl (dev.ud-speed) 12))))
    in xdiag-put (base + 3) buf

Section: Hub Requests

 Hub control lives on endpoint zero: the hub descriptor (class type
 0x29) states the port count, and each downstream port answers
 GET_STATUS with a status word and a change word. Every request here
 is a standard-shaped class transfer -- recipient interface for the
 descriptor, recipient other (the port) for features and status.

  hub-feat-reset : Integer = 4
  hub-feat-power : Integer = 8
  hub-feat-c-connection : Integer = 16
  hub-feat-c-reset : Integer = 20

  hub-find-interface : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hub-find-interface (cfg) (off) (total) =
    if off >= total then -1
    else let len = peek-byte cfg off
    in if len == 0 then -1
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 1) /= 4 then hub-find-interface cfg (off + len) total
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 5) /= 9 then hub-find-interface cfg (off + len) total
    else off

  hub-port-count : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer -> Integer
  hub-port-count (xh) (dev) (buf) =
    let g = xhci-ctrl-in xh dev 160 6 10496 0 buf 9
    in if g == False then 0 else peek-byte buf 2

 wHubCharacteristics is bytes 3 and 4 of the same descriptor, and its
 TT Think Time sub-field is bits 6:5 of the low byte (USB2 Table 11-13):
 0, 1, 2 and 3 mean the translator needs at most 8, 16, 24 or 32
 full-speed bit times of inter-transaction gap.

  hub-think-time : Integer -> Integer
  hub-think-time (buf) = bit-and (bit-shru (peek-byte buf 3) 5) 3

  hub-set-feature : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  hub-set-feature (xh) (dev) (port) (feat) =
    xhci-ctrl-nodata xh dev 35 3 feat port

  hub-clear-feature : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  hub-clear-feature (xh) (dev) (port) (feat) =
    xhci-ctrl-nodata xh dev 35 1 feat port

 Port status is returned as one little-endian dword: the status word
 low, the change word high. A failed transfer reads as -1, which no
 real status can be.

  hub-port-status : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hub-port-status (xh) (dev) (port) (buf) =
    let g = xhci-ctrl-in xh dev 163 0 0 port buf 4
    in if g == False then -1 else peek-32 buf 0

  hub-speed : Integer -> Integer
  hub-speed (status) =
    if bit-and status 512 /= 0 then 2
    else if bit-and status 1024 /= 0 then 3
    else 1

Section: Hub Bring-Up

 The controller must be told a slot is a hub before anything is
 addressed through it: Configure Endpoint evaluates the Hub flag and
 the port count from the input slot context. The command also
 declares the hub's one interrupt IN endpoint -- the status-change
 pipe every hub carries -- which keeps the command fully formed even
 though this driver polls port status by control request instead.

 xHCI 1.2c 6.2.2.2: "If Hub = '1' and Speed = High-Speed, then the TT
 Think Time (TTT) and Multi-TT (MTT) fields shall be initialized." TTT
 is Slot Context dword two bits 17:16 (Table 6-6) and 4.5.2 names its
 source as the hub descriptor's wHubCharacteristics. Zero is not a
 neutral default there: it asserts the tightest gap the field can
 express, and the xHC schedules split transactions to that claim.

 MTT stays zero, and that is the required value rather than an
 omission: Table 6-4 bit 25 and 4.5.2 both condition MTT = '1' on the
 hub's Multi-TT interface having been enabled by a Set Interface
 request, which nothing here issues, so the hub is operating its
 single translator. If Set Interface is ever added, MTT must be raised
 here AND on every child's slot context in the same change.

  hub-declare : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  hub-declare (xh) (dev) (cfg) (iface) (total) (ports) (ttt) =
    let ep = kbd-find-int-in cfg (iface + peek-byte cfg iface) total
    in if ep < 0 then False
    else let epn = bit-and (peek-byte cfg (ep + 2)) 15
    in let dci = epn * 2 + 1
    in let ring = xhci-ring-new
    in let r0 = xhci-set-ep-ring (dev.ud-ctx) dci (xhci-ring-trbs ring) (xh.xh-ctxsz)
    in let ictx = xhci-ictx-single dci 7 (kbd-ep-maxpkt cfg ep) (xhci-ring-trbs ring) (xhci-ep-interval (dev.ud-speed) (peek-byte cfg (ep + 6))) (xh.xh-ctxsz) (dev.ud-ctx)
    in let sb = xh.xh-ctxsz
    in let h0 = poke-32 ictx sb (bit-or (peek-32 ictx sb) (bit-shl 1 26))
    in let h1 = poke-32 ictx (sb + 4) (bit-or (bit-and (peek-32 ictx (sb + 4)) #00FFFFFF) (bit-shl ports 24))
    in let tt = if (dev.ud-speed) == 3 then ttt else 0
    in let h2 = poke-32 ictx (sb + 8) (bit-or (bit-and (peek-32 ictx (sb + 8)) #FFFCFFFF) (bit-shl tt 16))
    in let ce = xhci-configure-endpoint xh dev ictx
    in if ce.xe-ok == False then False
    else ce.xe-code == trb-cc-success

 Every port is powered before any is examined, so the mandatory
 power-good interval of the later ports elapses while the earlier
 ones are walked. The waits below are budgets of whole control
 transfers, each a real round-trip on the wire; exhausting one FAILS
 the port -- the floating-bus rule: garbage status must never be
 acted on as if it had been earned.

  usb-max-depth : Integer = 5
  hub-connect-fuel : Integer = 256
  hub-reset-fuel : Integer = 256

  hub-power-all : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hub-power-all (xh) (dev) (port) (count) =
    if port > count then 0
    else let s = hub-set-feature xh dev port hub-feat-power
    in hub-power-all xh dev (port + 1) count

  hub-wait-connect : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hub-wait-connect (xh) (dev) (port) (buf) (fuel) =
    let st = hub-port-status xh dev port buf
    in if st < 0 then 0
    else if bit-and st 1 /= 0 then 1
    else if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else hub-wait-connect xh dev port buf (fuel - 1)

  hub-wait-reset : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hub-wait-reset (xh) (dev) (port) (buf) (fuel) =
    let st = hub-port-status xh dev port buf
    in if st < 0 then -1
    else if bit-and st 1048576 /= 0 then st
    else if fuel <= 0 then -1
    else hub-wait-reset xh dev port buf (fuel - 1)

Section: Hub Walk

  usb-open-hub : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean, UsbFound -> UsbFound
  usb-open-hub (xh) (dev) (cfg) (iface) (total) (route) (depth) (cfgd) (found) =
    let sc = if cfgd then True else usb-set-configuration xh dev (peek-byte cfg 5)
    in if sc == False then found
    else let buf = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in let count = hub-port-count xh dev buf
    in if count == 0 then found
    else let hb = hub-declare xh dev cfg iface total count (hub-think-time buf)
    in if hb == False then found
    else let pw = hub-power-all xh dev 1 count
    in hub-walk xh dev buf 1 count route depth found

  hub-walk : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, UsbFound -> UsbFound
  hub-walk (xh) (hub) (buf) (port) (count) (route) (depth) (found) =
    if port > count then found
    else if port > 15 then found
    else let f2 = hub-attach-port xh hub buf port route depth found
    in hub-walk xh hub buf (port + 1) count route depth f2

 Reset settles a connected port and reveals the device's speed; the
 change bits are acknowledged so the next observer sees a quiet
 port. The downstream device is then addressed at the extended route,
 through a transaction translator when the speeds demand one, and
 inspected like any other -- including as a further hub.

 Which translator is not always this hub's. A translator lives in a
 high-speed hub; a full-speed hub has none, and everything below it
 is served by whichever high-speed hub sits further up. So the choice
 is by the hub's own speed, not by the child's: a high-speed hub
 translates for its full-speed child at the port that child occupies,
 and a hub that is not high-speed hands down the translator that
 already serves it. Reading only the immediate parent leaves a device
 two tiers down with no translator at all, which enumerates perfectly
 and then never delivers an interrupt transfer.

  hub-attach-port : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, UsbFound -> UsbFound
  hub-attach-port (xh) (hub) (buf) (port) (route) (depth) (found) =
    let c = hub-wait-connect xh hub port buf hub-connect-fuel
    in if c == 0 then found
    else let r = hub-set-feature xh hub port hub-feat-reset
    in if r == False then found
    else let st = hub-wait-reset xh hub port buf hub-reset-fuel
    in if st < 0 then found
    else let a0 = hub-clear-feature xh hub port hub-feat-c-reset
    in let a1 = hub-clear-feature xh hub port hub-feat-c-connection
    in if bit-and st 2 == 0 then found
    else let speed = hub-speed st
    in let ttslot = if speed == 3 then 0 else if hub.ud-speed == 3 then hub.ud-slot else hub.ud-tt-slot
    in let ttport = if speed == 3 then 0 else if hub.ud-speed == 3 then port else hub.ud-tt-port
    in let croute = bit-or route (bit-shl port (depth * 4))
    in let dev = xhci-open-device-at xh croute (hub.ud-port) speed ttslot ttport
    in if dev.ud-ok == False then found
    else usb-inspect xh dev croute (depth + 1) found
